==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|ἕτερος||different}} + {{m|grc|λόγος||word}}, {{surf|en|hetero-|logical|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-heterological.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|grammar}} Of a word, not [[describing]] itself.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993 |title=The Human Being as a Logical Thinker  |author=Noel Balzer -  |page=70 |ISBN=9051835205 |passage=A paradox arises when we ask, "Is the word 'heterological' '''heterological'''." If it is, it is not. If it is not, it is. }}
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} Not true of itself.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880 |title=Mind - Volume 5  |author= |page=144 |ISBN= |passage=A further instance of the uncertainty of Prof. Bergmann's views on the subject of negation, is the distinction (considered by him as at least supposable) between " nicht-richtig " and "unrichtig" (§21,5.). This is done, of course, in order to put a " '''heterological''' " meaning into his Axiom of Excluded Middle, }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994 |title=Truth and the Ethics of Criticism  |author=Christopher Norris  |page=50 |ISBN=0719044529 |passage=It is the dream of a purely '''heterological''' thought at its source. A pure thought of pure difference. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007 |title=Columbia Companion to Twentieth-century Philosophies  |author=Constantin V. Boundas  |page=489 |ISBN=0231142021 |passage=But a process philosophy, in order to support a purely '''heterological''' thought, has to be capable of doing without subjects steering the process or being steered by it, without substantive names designating 'blocks' in motion, and without points of origin or destination invigilating over a predictable trajectory. }}
# {{lb|en|biology}} Having the potential to develop into different forms.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884 |title=The American Naturalist  |author= |page=675 |ISBN= |passage=It is double and '''heterological''' in the middle organs or passages. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920 |title=Science - Volume 52  |author= |page=181 |ISBN= |passage=The normally situated non-transplanted gonads have the best chance of being able to absorb these substances, for which reason '''heterological''' (perhaps also homological) gonads, transplanted on normal organisms, can not get enough of these substances and therefore perish. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|title=Cell Therapy: A New Dimension of Medicine  |author=Franz Schmid  |page=106 |ISBN= |passage=Growth indices from density and extension of the zones of emigration in explants of rat bone-marrow on addition of various '''heterological''' tissue as against the controls. }}
# Supporting or attracted to [[otherness]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992 |title=Against Architecture: The Writings of Georges Bataille  |author=Denis Hollier  |page=74 |ISBN=0262581132 |passage=That is why it could only have a multitude of names, could only be expressed by the lexical extravagance to which it inevitably leads '''heterological''' practice and, as such, does not escape the substitutive discourse. Eroticism is just one of the names of '''heterological''' impulse at work — Incomplete through the metonymic effect of the copula — throughout The Caesarean Incomplete. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007 |title=Georges Bataille |author=Stuart Kendall  |page= |ISBN=1861895895 |passage=Bataille's writings in mythical anthropology from the late 1920s already associated the name Sade with a '''heterological''' interpretation of erotic expenditure.  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013 |title=Heritage in the Digital Era: Cinematic Tourism and the Activist Cause |author=Rodanthi Tzanelli  |page= |ISBN=1136163360 |passage=This produced a bifurcated narrative of identity as otherness, a '''heterological''' trope akin to that we encounter in the ill-defined domain of the Caribbean (Sheller 2003, 2004). }}
# Having or supporting multiple interpretations.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003 |title=Heterological Ethnicity: Conceptualizing Identities in Ancient Greece |author=Johannes Siapkas  |page= |ISBN= |passage=Indeed, Godzich has situated de Certeau in a '''heterological''' tradition, a scholarly tradition in opposition to the hegemonical tradition which has dominated western intellectual thinking. Scholars in the '''heterological''' tradition relativise scholarship and point to the constraints enforced by the contemporary social context on the production of truth. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009 |title=Power, Voice and Subjectivity in Literature for Young Readers |author=Maria Nikolajeva  |page= |ISBN=1135238235 |passage= Postcolonial theory provides yet another binarity: (ethnic) majority/minority, or imperialistic/ indigenous, yet another aspect of power hierarchy. Therefore I choose in discussions of gender to speak of '''heterological''' rather than feminist or queer analysis. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|title=Seek the Peace of the City |author=Richard Bourne  |page= |ISBN=1556356420 |passage=By using this language we can express Yoder's vision of the church as a community that opens up these '''heterological''' spaces as partial significations of the coming Kingdom. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|title=Saintly Influence: Edith Wyschogrod and the Possibilities of Pholosophy of Religion |author=Eric Boynton; Martin Kavka  |page= |ISBN=0823230899 |passage=I will suggest that, if we press the logic of her argument in ways she does not, then either Wyschogrod or God (however named) appears ultimately responsible for training her book's reader in the practice of '''heterological''' history.  }}

